<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ariovistus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ariovistus was a Germanic leader of the Suebi who crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> around 70 BCE as an ally of the Sequani and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a>. After defeating the Aedui he settled his people on Sequanian land, and in 59 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> recognised him as king and friend. In 58 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> defeated him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and drove his army back across the Rhine.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ariovistus.jpg" alt="Ariovistus" width="216" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Johann Nepomuk Geiger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caesar_and_Ariovistus_(meeting_before_the_battle)_by_Johann_Nepomuk_Geiger_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pressed by the Sequani and <b>Ariovistus</b>, the Aedui called on Caesar in 58 BCE, but in 52 BCE they joined Vercingetorix, who was chosen supreme leader at Bibracte.</blockquote><a href="/state/aedui">Aedui</a></li><li><blockquote>The Marcomanni ('border men') first appear among <b>Ariovistus</b>' followers in 58 BCE, probably living near the upper Rhine and Main.</blockquote><a href="/state/marcomanni">Marcomanni</a></li><li><blockquote>To defeat the Aedui they hired the German leader <b>Ariovistus</b>, who then seized a third of their land, until Caesar drove him out in 58 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/sequani">Sequani</a></li><li><blockquote>The Suebi enter history in 58 BCE when Julius Caesar fought their leader <b>Ariovistus</b> in Alsace, after he had crossed the Rhine about 72 BCE to aid the Sequani.</blockquote><a href="/state/suebi">Suebi</a></li><li><blockquote>The Triboci appear in Caesar's account of the battle against <b>Ariovistus</b> in 58 BCE as one of the peoples in his army.</blockquote><a href="/state/triboci">Triboci</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.9,11.1&amp;year=-35" aria-label="Germania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4802 398 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ariovistus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5252 668h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5252 668h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.9,11.1&amp;year=-35">What was here in 35 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
